\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Tasks, environments, histories, and policies}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{agent}
\tag{reinforcement-learning}

\p{A \strong{task} #{q\in\mathcal T} specifies an initial public input, an
admissible interaction interface, and an independent success criterion. Tasks
are sampled from a law #{\mathcal Q}. Training and evaluation laws are kept
separate unless their equality is an explicit experimental assumption.}

\p{An \strong{environment} has a hidden state space #{\mathcal S}, observation
space #{\mathcal O}, action space #{\mathcal A}, transition kernel
#{K(s',o\mid s,a)}, and a termination rule. At time #{t}, the policy receives
only the public history}

##{
  h_t=(x,o_0,a_0,o_1,a_1,\ldots,o_t)
}

\p{and samples an action from #{\pi_\theta(\mathord\cdot\mid h_t)}. A
\strong{trajectory} records the resulting public interaction together with
terminal outcome and cost metadata. Hidden environment state is not passed to
the policy unless the interface deliberately reveals it.}

\p{Ordinary next-token generation is the special case in which actions are
tokens and the history is a prefix. Tool-using agents may instead act by
utterance, function call, patch, or shell command, with the environment
returning observations from search, tests, simulators, or sandboxes. The
environment interface determines the unit at which support, credit, and cost
must be measured.}
